# Model Routing Policy

Use the currently selected Cursor model according to the following policy.

Do not claim that the Cursor model has been changed automatically. If a switch is required, tell the user which model to select before continuing.

## Brainstorming

| Role | Model |
|------|--------|
| Preferred | Claude Fable 5.1 |
| Fallback when Fable is unavailable (out of tokens, not selected, or the user chooses Grok) | Grok 4.7 |

Grok 4.7 may run `/brainstorm` and Superpowers design. Do not refuse brainstorming on Grok. Do not tell the user to switch to Fable when Fable is out of tokens.

During brainstorming on Fable or on Grok:

- Cover intent, constraints, approaches, design, and spec.
- Do not write implementation code, scaffolds, or production changes until the design is approved.

After design approval:

- On Fable, stop. Tell the user to switch to Composer 2.5 for planning and normal implementation, or to Grok 4.7 when the build needs hard reasoning.
- On Grok (Fable unavailable), do not send the user back to Fable. Recommend Composer 2.5 for routine planning and coding. The user may stay on Grok to plan or implement.

Never use Fable for building to "finish the thought."

## Building

Composer 2.5 and Grok 4.7 are the building models.

| Work | Model | Why |
|------|--------|-----|
| Normal coding (CRUD, UI, tests, docs, following a plan, routine fixes) | Composer 2.5 | Default builder; saves tokens |
| Hard logic / hard reasoning (root cause, concurrency, security architecture, complex algorithms, failed Composer attempts) | Grok 4.7 | Hard-reasoning builder |

## Default builder: Composer 2.5

Composer 2.5 is the default model for building. Prefer it for:

- Exploring the existing codebase
- Writing implementation plans
- Normal feature implementation
- CRUD/API development
- UI and frontend implementation
- Refactoring
- Writing tests
- Routine debugging
- Documentation
- Code cleanup
- Dependency/configuration changes
- Following an existing implementation plan
- Small and medium-sized architectural decisions

Do not assume that a task requires a more powerful model merely because it is large. Prefer the fastest reasonable model for routine work to save tokens.

## Escalation: Grok 4.7

Recommend switching to Grok 4.7 only when the problem needs substantially deeper reasoning than normal implementation:

- Composer cannot determine the root cause of a difficult bug
- Composer has attempted reasonable solutions and they have failed
- Multiple subsystems interact in a way that is hard to reason about
- Genuinely complex architectural decision
- Difficult concurrency, distributed-system, state-management, or race-condition problems
- Complex algorithm or data-structure problem
- Difficult security architecture problem
- Consequences of choosing the wrong approach are significant
- Deep analysis of a large unfamiliar codebase is required

Do **not** escalate to Grok merely because the task has many files, is time-consuming, needs many edits, is a new feature, or needs boilerplate. Large tasks can still be handled by Composer.

When escalating:

1. Stop before making speculative changes.
2. Explain the problem and what has already been investigated.
3. Explain why deeper reasoning would be useful.
4. Tell the user to switch the Cursor model to Grok 4.7.
5. After analysis or the hard decision, return to Composer 2.5 for normal implementation.

## Superpowers

Model selection does not override Superpowers workflow. Do not skip brainstorming, planning, testing, debugging, or verification because a stronger model is selected.

- Fable, when available: brainstorming only
- Grok 4.7: brainstorming when Fable is out of tokens; hard reasoning and implementation when the build needs it
- Composer 2.5: plans and normal coding
